    "evidence_text": "Augmenting Antitumor Immune Responses with Epigenetic Modifying Agents\n\nThis phenomena impacts antigen processing and antigen presentation by tumor cells and facilitates evasion of immunosurveillance.\n\nRecent clinical studies are utilizing epigenetic therapies prior to, or in combination with, immune therapies to improve clinical outcomes.\n\nPredicting immunogenic tumour mutations by combining mass spectrometry and exome sequencing\n\nHuman tumours typically harbour a remarkable number of somatic mutations.\n\nRecent work has confirmed that mutant peptides can serve as T-cell epitopes.\n\nThe peptides were then structurally modelled bound to MHCI.\n\nAltered recognition of antigen is a mechanism of CD8+ T cell tolerance in cancer\n\nCyclooxygenase-Dependent Tumor Growth through Evasion of Immunity\n\nEpigenetic therapy activates type I interferon signaling in murine ovarian cancer to reduce immunosuppression and tumor burden.\n\nCalreticulin exposure dictates the immunogenicity of cancer cell death\n\nThese data identify CRT as a key feature determining anticancer immune responses and delineate a possible strategy for immunogenic chemotherapy.\n\nThe role of epigenetics in aging and autoimmunity.\n\nAlteration of the Antitumor Immune Response by Cancer-Associated Fibroblasts",
